      "nets": ["USB_DP", "USB_DN"],
      "observed": "R10 and R11 are 2.2k 1% resistors in series with the USB data lines.",
      "expected": "USB 2.0 series resistors should be approximately 22Ω (e.g., 22Ω or 27Ω) for impedance matching; 2.2kΩ is about 100× too high and will severely attenuate USB signals.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R10 2.2k 1%"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R11 2.2k 1%"}
      ],
      "impact": "USB communication on the affected ports will likely fail or be highly unreliable due to excessive series resistance.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["R17"],
      "pins": ["R17.1", "R17.2"],
      "nets": ["FB", "+12V"],
      "observed": "R17 is drawn with a resistor reference prefix (R) but has a capacitor value of 22pF, placed in the feedback network of the boost converter U12.",
      "expected": "A feedback feedforward capacitor in this location should be a capacitor, e.g., C17 22pF; a resistor with a 22pF value is inconsistent with the part type and function.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R17 22pF"}
      ],
      "impact": "If a resistor is fitted, the boost converter feedback network is incorrect and may oscillate or regulate poorly; if a capacitor is fitted, the BOM/reference designation is wrong.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U11", "U10"],
      "pins": ["U11.4", "U11.5", "U10.4", "U10.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The logic-side ground (GND1, pin 4) and bus-side ground (GND2, pin 5) of the isolated CAN transceivers U11 and U10 are both connected to the same GND net.",
      "expected": "For an isolated CAN transceiver, GND1 and GND2 must be separate isolated grounds to maintain galvanic isolation between the logic side and the CAN bus side.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "U11 GND1 pin 4 to GND; U11 GND2 pin 5 to GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The intended galvanic isolation between the logic ground and CAN bus ground is defeated, removing protection against ground loops and common-mode noise.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain several anomalies, most notably USB data-line series resistors R10/R11 at 2.2kΩ instead of the required ~22Ω, a capacitor-value component labeled as resistor R17 in the boost converter feedback, and isolated CAN transceiver grounds tied together. The USB resistor error is the most clearly supported and likely to cause USB failure."
}

